codin and percocet than  risk the liver damage that is caused by  ingesting too much acetaminophen it's a  new twist on an old fear prescription  drug abuse are you or someone you know  at  to prescription pills we hear about  famous people end up addicted to  painkillers they once used for  legitimate medical conditions I took the  pain pills from a car accident for a leg  injury and PSA suggest that regular  people get addicted all the time and  then I tried to stop but it was just way  too addictive blood all over me from  shooting up and so you might be shocked  to hear gardeners say this I didn't  become addicted and it was very easy to  go off of them as long as I could could  you know do it in a controlled manner  surely he must be the exception that  proves the rule the fear that people  will become addicted in large numbers  for medical treatment is just hype is  propaganda dr. Frank Fisher is a Harvard  trained physician he says these people  are the exceptions when patients take  control substances particularly opioid  analgesics for chronic pain the  addiction rate is incredibly low in 2008  the Official Journal of the American  Academy of pain medicine published a  review of studies on the topic what did  the review find among patients who were  prescribed pain medication and had no  prior history of drug abuse the abuse or  addiction rate was less than one-fifth  of 1% the nation of zombies but that's  not the story we hear on TV woke up  three days later covered in my own feces  or in a blind panic about these supposed  threats and they're very small compared  to most other things that actually  threaten our society the FDA panel says  458 people die each year from  acetaminophen overdoses and that  justifies banning medication like  vicodin and percocet but nearly 700  people die from drowning and swimming  pools each year and more than 1,800 died  falling downstairs should we ban pools  and stairs all medications are dangerous  we got to remember th